# This Old Man: traditional English counting song.
{title: This Old Man}
{composer: Traditional (England)}
{key: G}
{tempo: 112}
{time: 4/4}
{x_level: 1}
{x_strum: D - D - D - D -}

{comment: Quarter-note downs to start; later try D - D U - U D U.}

{start_of_grid label="Verse"}
| G . . . | G . . . |
| C . . . | D . . . |
| G . . . | G . . . |
| D . . . | G . . . |
{end_of_grid}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
[G]This old man, he played one,
He played [C]knick-knack on my [D]thumb,
With a [G]knick-knack, paddywhack,
This [D]old man came rolling [G]home.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
[G]This old man, he played two,
He played [C]knick-knack on my [D]shoe,
With a [G]knick-knack, paddywhack,
This [D]old man came rolling [G]home.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
[G]This old man, he played three,
He played [C]knick-knack on my [D]knee,
With a [G]knick-knack, paddywhack,
This [D]old man came rolling [G]home.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 4"}
[G]This old man, he played four,
He played [C]knick-knack on my [D]door,
With a [G]knick-knack, paddywhack,
This [D]old man came rolling [G]home.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: G}
